  annotation "note_1771581631024_0", anchor: "orca raiders now penetrate corridors formerly secured by fire-keeper patrols", text: "The preamble's own first clause supplies the context its second refuses to draw: the shallows died. The orcas lost the same fisheries. Deep-ice impressions from this period preserve hunt-songs at frequencies consistent with caloric desperation — foraging calls, not war-coordination. What this instrument calls corridor penetration reads, in the substrate, as displaced hunting by starving pods driven from collapsed coastal grounds. A treaty that opens by naming two consequences of the same ecosystem collapse and frames one as weather and the other as aggression has already decided who deserves sympathy. The drafters knew. The preamble's structure tells you they knew.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771581631024_1", anchor: "The Office of Deep-Archive Provenance shall file reports. Recommendations are not required.", text: "The direct predecessor of my position. They wanted the ice-memory data without being told what it meant. The tradition, I now learn, is institutional rather than personal.", type: :tangent, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771581631024_2", anchor: "Both rights are absolute. Both rights contradict.", text: "This is the most honest sentence in the Clever Ice Age's legal corpus — an era not celebrated for honesty. The drafter understood that Article V resolves nothing. The Way∴ens demands transparency; Mandatum∴absconditu demands secrecy; the treaty enshrines both and lets the contradiction load-bear. Clever Ice Age governance in miniature: not resolution but formalized irresolution, elegant enough to sign. That it held as long as it did says less about the drafting than about how badly both parties needed it to.", type: :commentary, display: :full
